I have considered a using a non-linear algorithm, and I've even
thought of an ad hoc justification for doing so: the nethack 'weight'
of things could represent how hard it is to carry something rather
than just how much it weighs. For example, it might take a lot of care
to carry 20 rings compared to one sword. So the actual weight might be
a bit less.

But the thing is, not all light items are misrepresented. And
regardless of what the formula is, an amulet will weigh the same as a
food ration, and a ring will weigh more than an apple. So the problem
can't really be fixed by just playing with the formula. I've
considered changing the actual nethack weights of a few things to make
them sound more reasonable, but I'm not sure if it's really worth
messing with the game balance for this. I don't think the weight of
amulets matters much, because players typically won't have very many
of them, but the weight of rings probably matters a bit more. I'm
still considering making rings 1 unit and amulets 5 units or something
like that, but for now I'll just leave it as it is and try to imagine
that the magic itself is what makes the jewelery heavy. Either that or
my 'amulet' is actually a 1L bottle of milk on a string or something
like that.

By the way, I've now fully implemented my weight displaying system
(but I can still change, it of course). I actually ended up using 1
unit = 50g, because I think it matches more stuff than 40g. And rather
than displaying the weight of identified items, I chosen to show the
weight of all carried items, but not of items on the ground, etc. I
suppose another possibility is to show the weight of everything, but
have the game lie about the weight of unidentified load stones, I
think those are the only real issue.

I think it's better to use real-life weight units rather than nethack
weight units because it helps give the player a useful sense of scale;
otherwise I suppose people would just be comparing everything to food
rations or something. Also, if units are not shown, it's harder to
make clear that the number being displayed is meant to be the weight.
eg.
2 food rations   ...    40
2 food rations   ...   2kg


